## 14.1 Introduction

Memory management is arguably the most intricate subsystem in the Linux kernel. Every allocation, every page fault, and every reclaim decision flows through layers of abstraction that have evolved over three decades. This chapter examines three pillars of that subsystem: the **slab allocator** (a slab-derived caching layer), the **page table** structures that map virtual to physical memory, and the **NUMA balancing** machinery that keeps memory access local to the CPU that needs it. Throughout, we provide annotated code examples drawn from the kernel's own idioms.

> **Note on terminology:** The names used here — "slab allocator," "NUMA," "page tables" — follow the conventions of this text. In mainline Linux you will encounter `slab` as `slab`, `NUMA` as `NUMA`, and page tables as `page tables`. The concepts are identical; only the spelling differs.

## 14.2 The Physical Memory Model

Before any allocator can function, the kernel must know what memory exists. During boot, the firmware (BIOS or EFI) reports memory regions via the **EFI memory map**. The kernel parses this map into a `memblk` array:

```c
struct memblk {
    unsigned long start_pfn;   /* starting page frame number */
    unsigned long end_pfn;     /* one past the last usable frame */
    unsigned int   numa_nid;   /* NUMA node identifier */
    unsigned int   flags;      /* MEMBLK_HOTPLUG, MEMBLK_KERNEL, ... */
};

static struct memblk embmap[MEMMAP_MAX];
static unsigned int embmap_nr;
```

Each block is later handed to the buddy allocator, which maintains free lists of order-0 through order-10 page groups. The buddy system is the bedrock: it satisfies large contiguous requests and fragments gracefully under pressure. Everything else — slab caches, vmmalloc, page tables — sits atop it.

A crucial invariant: **physical page frames are numbered globally**, but their NUMA affinity is per-block. The function `pfn_to_nid()` performs the translation:

```c
static inline int pfn_to_nid(unsigned long pfn)
{
    struct memblk *b = &embmap[0];
    while (b < &embmap[embmap_nr]) {
        if (pfn >= b->start_pfn && pfn < b->end_pfn)
            return b->numa_nid;
        b++;
    }
    return NUMA_NO_NID;
}
```

