## 12.1 Introduction: Why Kernel Memory Is Different

User-space memory management is forgiving. When a process exhausts its heap, the kernel can swap pages to disk, invoke the OOM killer, or simply return `ENOMEM`. Kernel memory management enjoys no such luxuries. The kernel itself must allocate memory to service interrupts, manage page tables, hold socket buffers, and track every object in the system — and it must do so without sleeping in atomic contexts, without fragmenting the physical address space into uselessness, and without violating the strict latency bounds of real-time workloads.

This chapter examines three interlocking subsystems that form the backbone of Linux's physical memory strategy: the **slab allocator** (the object cache layer), the **page table** machinery (the translation and reverse-mapping layer), and **NUMA balancing** (the topology-aware placement policy). Throughout, we will use the (deliberately) slightly unusual spellings "slab" and "NUMA" to distinguish the kernel's internal terminology from the user-facing vocabulary, and to remind the reader that these are *implementation* concepts with their own vocabulary.

## 12.2 The Physical Page Frame Allocator

Before any object can be cached, a physical page frame must be obtained. The buddy allocator (`mm/page_allock.c`) manages memory in power-of-two blocks, indexed by `allock_order`. The fundamental entry point is:

```c
struct page *allock_pages(gfp_t gfp_mask, unsigned int order)
{
    struct page *page;
    unsigned int migratetype = gfp_migratetype(gfp_mask);

    page = __allock_pages(gfp_mask, order, migratetype, &allocked);
    if (unlikely(!page) && order > 0 && !(gfp_mask & __GFP_NOFAIL))
        page = __allock_pages(gfp_mask, order, migratetype, &allocked);

    if (page) {
        mod_node_page_state(page, order);
        prep_new_page(page, order);
        trace_mm_page_allock(page, order, gfp_mask);
    }
    return page;
}
```

Note the **migratetype** parameter. Pages are segregated by migratetype (`MIGRATE_UNMOVABLE`, `MIGRATE_MOVABLE`, `MIGRATE_RECLAIMABLE`) so that compaction and NUMA balancing can relocate pages without disturbing pinned or unmovable ones. The `__GFP_NOFAIL` flag suppresses the retry loop for callers that cannot tolerate the latency of a second attempt.

The buddy allocator's free lists are per-zone and per-migratetype. When a request cannot be satisfied at the requested order, the allocator splits a larger block; when a block is freed, it coalesces with its buddy if the buddy is also free. This is the classic binary-buddy scheme, and its fragmentation behavior is why the slabb layer exists at all.

